1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:05,000
Killer chemicals ooze from a toxic waste dump into a residential neighborhood.

2
00:00:06,500 --> 00:00:08,108
World Trade Center Building No.

3
00:00:08,109 --> 00:00:12,000
7 is reduced to a pile of rubble on September 11th, but it wasn't attacked.

4
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:16,000
Patriot missile software problems leave U.S. soldiers defenseless.

5
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:21,000
A nightclub turns into an inferno that kills over 400 people.

6
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:26,000
And the science of building implosion is put to the test and fails.

7
00:00:26,001 --> 00:00:30,000
Now, Engineering Disasters on Modern Marbles.

8
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:44,000
World Trade Center Building No. 7.

9
00:00:44,001 --> 00:00:50,000
On September 11, 2001, it was one of the casualties of the terrorist attacks on that horrific day.

10
00:00:50,001 --> 00:00:56,180
Building 7's sudden and shocking collapse has been overshadowed by the fate of the Twin Towers.

11
00:00:57,020 --> 00:00:59,920
But from an engineering standpoint, it is the greater mystery.

12
00:01:01,280 --> 00:01:04,640
We were surprised that Tower 7 collapsed,

13
00:01:05,500 --> 00:01:09,580
we being the team that investigated what occurred on that day.

14
00:01:09,581 --> 00:01:16,420
There was some damage to Tower 7 caused by the debris that hit it from Tower 1,

15
00:01:16,421 --> 00:01:22,800
but the damage was certainly not similar in scope or magnitude

16
00:01:22,801 --> 00:01:26,080
to that caused by the aircraft hitting towers 1 and 2.

17
00:01:26,980 --> 00:01:29,540
The only positive aspect of this building's demise

18
00:01:29,541 --> 00:01:32,360
is that it fell seven hours after the two main towers.

19
00:01:33,820 --> 00:01:35,420
Everyone had been evacuated

20
00:01:35,421 --> 00:01:40,080
and even though the 47-story building collapsed in on itself in under 10 seconds,

21
00:01:40,960 --> 00:01:41,800
no one was killed.

22
00:01:43,180 --> 00:01:45,220
World Trade Center 7 was completed in 1987.

23
00:01:45,221 --> 00:01:51,860
It had approximately 1.8 million square feet, 47 stories, and was roughly 600 feet tall.

24
00:01:52,340 --> 00:01:57,100
Each of the floors was approximately 40,000 square feet, which is roughly the size of a football field

25
00:01:57,101 --> 00:02:00,480
and equal to the size of the floor plate on each of the towers.

26
00:02:01,520 --> 00:02:06,020
The building was a good building. It was soundly designed.

27
00:02:07,440 --> 00:02:14,500
It would still be standing if not for 9-11, and it was state-of-the-art at the time, absolutely.

28
00:02:15,220 --> 00:02:19,220
One of the challenges the engineers faced in designing World Trade Center 7

29
00:02:19,221 --> 00:02:23,780
was finding a way to erect the building over an electric company substation

30
00:02:23,781 --> 00:02:26,760
that already existed on the north side of the site.

31
00:02:27,760 --> 00:02:31,440
As a result, we had to do very many, I'll use the word tricks,

32
00:02:32,260 --> 00:02:38,480
transferring of column loads, carrying loads either on cantilevers or on truss systems.

33
00:02:40,440 --> 00:02:42,760
It was a challenging project at the time.

34
00:02:42,761 --> 00:02:49,760
Analysis of the collapse of Number 7, which had been burning out of control since the collapse of Tower 1 seven hours earlier,

35
00:02:49,761 --> 00:02:55,760
seems to indicate the initial failure occurred somewhere between the fifth and seventh floors.

36
00:02:55,761 --> 00:03:01,760
This is where the transfer trusses were built to carry the load over the Con Edison substation.

37
00:03:01,761 --> 00:03:08,760
The way the building fell supports the idea that at least one of the trusses was weakened by the fires in the building and gave way.

38
00:03:08,761 --> 00:03:16,360
Once one of those trusses went down, then because of the way everything was interconnected

39
00:03:16,361 --> 00:03:22,160
in the floor system, it pulled down the rest of the building.

40
00:03:22,161 --> 00:03:29,720
Normally when you have a structural failure, you carefully go through the debris field

41
00:03:29,721 --> 00:03:35,920
looking at each item, photographing every beam as it collapsed and every column where

42
00:03:35,921 --> 00:03:40,920
it is in the ground and you pick them up very carefully and you look at each element.

43
00:03:41,680 --> 00:03:44,240
We were unable to do that in the case of Tower 7.

44
00:03:45,540 --> 00:03:49,900
The wreckage from Tower 7 was mixed in with the debris from the other destroyed or damaged

45
00:03:49,901 --> 00:03:55,000
buildings and removed from the site so rescue and recovery efforts could continue.

46
00:03:55,700 --> 00:03:58,880
We then have to ask, why did the building fail on 9-11?

47
00:03:59,180 --> 00:04:01,380
Well, there are a couple of things we could look at.

48
00:04:01,381 --> 00:04:05,880
One is perhaps the column didn't have the right amount of fireproofing.

49
00:04:05,881 --> 00:04:09,480
Well, we have no indication that this was a substandard building at all.

50
00:04:09,481 --> 00:04:15,480
It is certainly possible that the debris that started the fire in Tower 7,

51
00:04:15,481 --> 00:04:18,680
this is the debris from Tower 1 when it collapsed into Tower 7,

52
00:04:18,681 --> 00:04:21,780
that that debris knocked off some of the fireproofing.

53
00:04:21,781 --> 00:04:26,980
It doesn't take much fireproofing to be removed for the steel to fail.

54
00:04:26,981 --> 00:04:32,360
One theory suggests the steel around the fifth floor was superheated by diesel fuel,

55
00:04:32,600 --> 00:04:36,180
discharged from broken pipes, supplying the building's emergency generators.

56
00:04:36,980 --> 00:04:38,600
But the explanation may be simpler.

57
00:04:39,420 --> 00:04:43,780
The building caught fire at 10.30 a.m. when Tower 1 collapsed.

58
00:04:44,780 --> 00:04:47,760
The water pipes supplying the sprinkler systems were also damaged.

59
00:04:49,040 --> 00:04:55,600
And the fire department made the decision to allow an evacuated building to burn for the next seven hours

60
00:04:55,601 --> 00:05:00,260
in favor of trying to save the lives of those trapped in the rubble of the Twin Towers.

61
00:05:03,140 --> 00:05:08,760
If you allow it to burn long enough, it's going to get past the fireproofing material,

62
00:05:09,240 --> 00:05:12,380
and it's going to cook the steel, and the steel is going to yield.

63
00:05:12,900 --> 00:05:15,200
To ensure against future high-rise disasters,

64
00:05:15,820 --> 00:05:20,400
many different agencies are involved in the ongoing effort to determine the exact cause of the failure.

65
00:05:21,560 --> 00:05:24,300
However, this has not stopped the construction of a new building.

66
00:05:24,301 --> 00:05:29,800
We have to rebuild on that site. We have to rebuild on the World Trade Center site.

67
00:05:31,380 --> 00:05:38,120
If we didn't, then we lose. And we ain't going to lose.

68
00:05:39,120 --> 00:05:45,503
Just a little more than a year after 9-11, construction began on a new World Trade Center No.

69
00:05:45,504 --> 00:05:46,204
7.

70
00:05:46,420 --> 00:05:49,860
Since engineers knew it was to be the first building to rise from the ashes,

71
00:05:49,861 --> 00:05:55,260
They made safety features a top priority in its design.

72
00:05:55,920 --> 00:06:01,180
It's going to be 52 stories when it's completed, 750 feet tall and all.

73
00:06:01,900 --> 00:06:05,860
Up above us you see, and all around this floor, you see fireproofing.

74
00:06:06,400 --> 00:06:13,120
What we've done that's different, that's beyond the code, and indeed beyond normal practice in buildings,

75
00:06:13,220 --> 00:06:18,300
is we've made the fireproofing five times as durable as required by code.

76
00:06:18,301 --> 00:06:21,800
and fireproofing can withstand all kinds of circumstances,

77
00:06:21,801 --> 00:06:24,500
and that's a very important safety development.

78
00:06:25,320 --> 00:06:28,120
The stairs are 20% wider than required,

79
00:06:28,540 --> 00:06:30,680
which will allow people to evacuate quicker

80
00:06:30,681 --> 00:06:34,800
and emergency teams to move more freely within the building.

81
00:06:36,040 --> 00:06:37,540
Beyond standard emergency lighting,

82
00:06:38,100 --> 00:06:41,220
the stairways will have track lights on the floor similar to airliners,

83
00:06:41,560 --> 00:06:44,020
and if that fails, all the railings and stairs

84
00:06:44,021 --> 00:06:47,480
are being marked with photoluminescent paint that glows in the dark.

85
00:06:48,300 --> 00:06:58,795
Probably the single most important safety innovation in this building is that all of the life safety systems of this building are encased in a massive, two-foot-thick,

86
00:06:58,796 --> 00:07:00,680
in most places, concrete wall.

87
00:07:00,681 --> 00:07:12,244
That assures that in the event of an emergency, the life safety systems that support a quick and successful evacuation, the stairs, the water standpipes that support

88
00:07:12,245 --> 00:07:17,500
sprinkling, emergency lighting, emergency communication, are all protected.

89
00:07:17,501 --> 00:07:27,820
The concrete that's being used in the core and elsewhere in the building is what you call high-strength concrete, similar to what's used in military bunkers.

90
00:07:28,580 --> 00:07:34,560
The new World Trade Center 7 building, like the original, is being built over the Con Ed substation.

91
00:07:35,260 --> 00:07:37,940
But the structural load is being handled in a different way.

92
00:07:38,600 --> 00:07:49,980
The structure of the exterior of the building relates to the Connett substation with a belt wall that takes place just above the level of the substation,

93
00:07:50,240 --> 00:07:57,160
where we transfer the loads from the office building so that they're distributed throughout the exterior of the Connett substation at the base.

94
00:07:57,840 --> 00:08:02,240
There was pride and determination in the builders of the new World Trade Center 7.

95
00:08:02,960 --> 00:08:05,180
Scheduled for completion in early 2006,

96
00:08:05,181 --> 00:08:12,640
It represents the finest in current building design and is symbolic of the resilience of a nation.

97
00:08:16,820 --> 00:08:20,421
Tenants of World Trade Center 7 included the IRS, the U.S.

98
00:08:20,422 --> 00:08:24,580
Secret Service, and New York City's Office of Emergency Management.

99
00:08:25,800 --> 00:08:28,740
Engineering Disasters will return on Modern Marvels.

100
00:08:30,160 --> 00:08:33,660
We now return to Engineering Disasters on Modern Marvels.

101
00:08:35,180 --> 00:08:48,841
Just east of Niagara Falls in northwest New York State, an area with the deceptively serene name of Love Canal turned out to be one of America's most notorious toxic

102
00:08:48,842 --> 00:08:50,580
waste horror stories.

103
00:08:51,920 --> 00:08:58,960
An inadequate cover on a chemical company's dump site allowed a deadly brew of toxins to leak into a suburban neighborhood.

104
00:09:00,100 --> 00:09:01,800
A public health scare erupted.

105
00:09:02,160 --> 00:09:03,640
How can you make decisions?

106
00:09:03,641 --> 00:09:07,620
Are you going to live there with your wife? Are you going to put your kids there?

107
00:09:08,480 --> 00:09:10,980
Over 900 families left their homes.

108
00:09:12,680 --> 00:09:18,660
Several years and hundreds of millions of dollars had to be spent to try to stop the further spread of the contaminants.

109
00:09:20,020 --> 00:09:24,120
The Love Canal originally got its name from entrepreneur William T. Love.

110
00:09:24,860 --> 00:09:32,720
In 1894, he began construction on a man-made canal that he envisioned would link the Niagara River to Lake Ontario.

111
00:09:33,640 --> 00:09:38,760
His plan was to use the power of the river as it rushes towards the falls,

112
00:09:38,920 --> 00:09:41,020
diverting it before it goes over the falls,

113
00:09:41,480 --> 00:09:43,440
and carrying it north through a canal,

114
00:09:43,720 --> 00:09:46,540
which would wind up at this escarpment or cliff

115
00:09:46,541 --> 00:09:50,800
and pour down and provide the power that's necessary to generate electricity.

116
00:09:51,760 --> 00:09:55,200
The canal was essentially a trench dug in the clay soil of the area.

117
00:09:56,100 --> 00:09:58,260
After only 3,000 feet were excavated,

118
00:09:59,200 --> 00:10:02,160
a pair of unforeseen events brought construction to a halt.

119
00:10:03,160 --> 00:10:06,660
One was the depression of the 1890s, severe depression.

120
00:10:06,900 --> 00:10:14,420
Secondly was somebody named Tesla devised a way to have alternating current instead of direct current

121
00:10:14,421 --> 00:10:16,900
so you didn't have to be right on top of the source of power.

122
00:10:17,480 --> 00:10:22,960
Alternating current made it unnecessary to develop industry and Love's planned city adjacent to his canal.

123
00:10:24,620 --> 00:10:30,360
For the next several years, Love's unfinished canal served as a summer swimming hole for nearby residents.

124
00:10:31,220 --> 00:10:35,820
Then in 1942, Hooker Chemical Company acquired the canal.

125
00:10:36,780 --> 00:10:43,960
They ended up using the property as pretty much a 16-acre hole in the ground at that point,

126
00:10:44,680 --> 00:10:47,140
3,000 feet long by 80 feet wide to 100 feet wide,

127
00:10:47,740 --> 00:10:51,860
probably anywhere from 8 to 15 or 20 feet in depth.

128
00:10:52,540 --> 00:10:55,900
And they used it for disposal of industrial waste

129
00:10:55,901 --> 00:11:00,400
from a period of time from about 1942 to 1952 or 1953.

130
00:11:01,240 --> 00:11:06,780
By 1953, the canal was filled with over 20,000 tons of chemical wastes,

131
00:11:07,040 --> 00:11:08,960
including caustic solvents and pesticides.

132
00:11:10,240 --> 00:11:12,020
Hooker had no further use for the canal,

133
00:11:12,640 --> 00:11:15,340
and with no regulations in place to stop them at the time,

134
00:11:15,800 --> 00:11:20,060
they simply covered the dump site with a layer of clay and soil from the area.

135
00:11:21,220 --> 00:11:25,220
Soon grass grew and helped hide what festered just a few feet below.

136
00:11:25,900 --> 00:11:29,294
Of course, everybody played on that area because it's not a canal.

137
00:11:29,295 --> 00:11:30,220
It's covered over.

138
00:11:30,720 --> 00:11:36,220
And sometimes kids would get, their feet would get irritated from running around in the canal.

139
00:11:36,820 --> 00:11:43,320
As Niagara Falls' growing population spread, the New York Board of Education was looking for inexpensive land

140
00:11:43,321 --> 00:11:46,460
and expressed interest in the property Hooker owned.

141
00:11:47,420 --> 00:11:52,440
The chemical company offered to sell the no longer useful land for $1.

142
00:11:52,760 --> 00:11:55,860
The deed did contain a warning about the contents of the canal.

143
00:11:55,900 --> 00:11:57,700
But that did not stop the sale.

144
00:11:58,600 --> 00:12:00,940
Well, they didn't build the school directly on top of the canal.

145
00:12:01,160 --> 00:12:02,660
They built a playground there.

146
00:12:03,040 --> 00:12:04,720
And on the black top of the playground,

147
00:12:05,080 --> 00:12:08,400
there was like a percolation of a taffy-like watery substance

148
00:12:08,401 --> 00:12:12,180
coming out of the ground into this playground.

149
00:12:13,240 --> 00:12:14,500
At around the same time,

150
00:12:14,700 --> 00:12:16,580
there continued to be increased development in the area.

151
00:12:17,040 --> 00:12:19,280
A series of homes went up around the canal.

152
00:12:19,780 --> 00:12:22,020
None of the homes were built right on the canal.

153
00:12:22,440 --> 00:12:23,800
They were adjacent to it.

154
00:12:23,801 --> 00:12:29,200
And then a lot of residents complained of not only smells in their basement,

155
00:12:29,700 --> 00:12:34,180
but of oozing of the similar types of chemicals into those sub-pumps,

156
00:12:34,580 --> 00:12:36,440
into the drainage ditches around their basements.

157
00:12:36,441 --> 00:12:41,780
The leachate that was found throughout the Love Canal community

158
00:12:41,781 --> 00:12:47,180
was generally a black goop, to use a technical, non-technical term.

159
00:12:47,880 --> 00:12:50,380
It was a very thick black material.

160
00:12:50,740 --> 00:12:52,120
It was very oily-based.

161
00:12:52,121 --> 00:12:54,720
It almost looked like a tar-like material.

162
00:12:55,680 --> 00:12:59,960
It was more watery in some instances than in others.

163
00:13:00,560 --> 00:13:03,840
Those who had bought homes in the area hadn't been warned in their deeds

164
00:13:03,841 --> 00:13:06,440
of what was beneath the grassy field.

165
00:13:07,320 --> 00:13:10,740
For new residents like Lois Gibbs, something was clearly wrong.

166
00:13:11,300 --> 00:13:15,440
In 1977, her son began attending the 99th Street School.

167
00:13:16,060 --> 00:13:19,780
I just remember after living at Love Canal for a while,

168
00:13:19,781 --> 00:13:22,940
Michael got sick, and he kept getting new diseases.

169
00:13:23,140 --> 00:13:26,680
He had asthma, liver problems, immune system problem,

170
00:13:27,120 --> 00:13:30,640
urinary tract disorder, which required two operations to correct.

171
00:13:31,060 --> 00:13:32,800
I mean, it was just like one thing after the other.

172
00:13:33,200 --> 00:13:36,140
And I could not figure out what was wrong with him.

173
00:13:36,580 --> 00:13:40,040
Around this time, a newspaper reporter wrote a story about the canal

174
00:13:40,041 --> 00:13:41,380
and what was buried in it.

175
00:13:42,200 --> 00:13:46,280
Now the residents had an explanation for the mysterious substances and illnesses,

176
00:13:46,980 --> 00:13:49,640
and Lois Gibbs decided something had to be done.

177
00:13:49,780 --> 00:13:54,080
First, she tried to have her child transferred out of the school.

178
00:13:54,820 --> 00:13:58,840
And they told her that they wouldn't do it because there was no problem.

179
00:13:59,160 --> 00:14:00,760
There was no chemical problem there.

180
00:14:01,800 --> 00:14:07,460
And so she started going up and down the street, talking to people, talking to neighbors,

181
00:14:07,461 --> 00:14:13,540
asking if they were interested in joining a little group that would try to get the school cleaned up.

182
00:14:13,980 --> 00:14:19,420
Awareness of the problem and complaints from the residents became much more widespread after the winter of 1977.

183
00:14:19,780 --> 00:14:28,880
It was the precipitation event, the blizzard of 77, that actually overfilled the canal,

184
00:14:29,100 --> 00:14:37,120
the bathtub effect, with all of the winter snow, etc., and the actual wet season that

185
00:14:37,121 --> 00:14:40,020
we had in 1977 after this blizzard.

186
00:14:40,021 --> 00:14:46,540
We had an early spring, an early thaw, and all the water was forced underneath the canal

187
00:14:46,541 --> 00:14:49,800
from this clay pit, basically, to the top,

188
00:14:49,860 --> 00:14:52,300
and it started oozing out of the top of the canal.

189
00:14:53,420 --> 00:14:57,880
In the late 1970s, there were no conclusive answers about the health risks

190
00:14:57,881 --> 00:15:01,560
and long-term effects of exposure to toxic wastes.

191
00:15:02,980 --> 00:15:05,740
Nobody was really sure what wastes were in the canal,

192
00:15:06,100 --> 00:15:08,520
but deadly dioxin was identified.

193
00:15:09,600 --> 00:15:12,900
The Environmental Protection Agency had been formed just a few years earlier

194
00:15:12,901 --> 00:15:16,120
and had not dealt with a public crisis like this before.

195
00:15:16,540 --> 00:15:20,580
the residents' fear turned to frustration and anger.

196
00:15:21,180 --> 00:15:24,200
We want us! We want us!

197
00:15:24,201 --> 00:15:27,820
The Love Canal became the focus of the growing environmental movement

198
00:15:27,821 --> 00:15:31,040
and was soon national news.

199
00:15:31,600 --> 00:15:35,380
On August 7, 1978, then-president Jimmy Carter

200
00:15:35,381 --> 00:15:37,720
declared Love Canal a disaster area

201
00:15:37,721 --> 00:15:42,020
and those living nearest to the dump site were relocated.

202
00:15:42,021 --> 00:15:52,940
Right after the first relocation in August of 1978, they erected a 10-foot fence, a green fence, so it would be environmentally pleasant, around Love Canal.

203
00:15:53,140 --> 00:15:58,320
And then they put signs on the fence that said, hazardous area, dangerous, do not enter.

204
00:15:59,060 --> 00:16:02,700
Infrared pictures were taken from the air to track the spread of the contaminants.

205
00:16:03,300 --> 00:16:06,640
Gray or black in the photos revealed the presence of the chemical waste.

206
00:16:06,641 --> 00:16:11,560
after another two years of activism by lois gibbs and other residents

207
00:16:11,561 --> 00:16:18,720
a second presidential declaration cleared the way for another 500 families to be relocated

208
00:16:18,721 --> 00:16:24,460
except for a few families who refused to move love canal became a ghost town

209
00:16:24,461 --> 00:16:33,080
continuing studies revealed the contents of the canal there were over 400 chemicals found in the

210
00:16:33,081 --> 00:16:34,680
leachate from the Love Canal Landfill.

211
00:16:35,020 --> 00:16:39,100
From lead and arsenic to lindane,

212
00:16:39,560 --> 00:16:40,840
a variety of other pesticides,

213
00:16:41,380 --> 00:16:45,780
to solvents such as benzene, toluene, xylene,

214
00:16:46,380 --> 00:16:47,220
a host of others.

215
00:16:48,100 --> 00:16:49,800
Many of these chemicals cause cancer.

216
00:16:50,340 --> 00:16:52,980
Some of them, many of them cause liver problems,

217
00:16:53,080 --> 00:16:53,780
liver disease.

218
00:16:54,320 --> 00:16:56,580
Many of them cause central nervous system problems.

219
00:16:56,760 --> 00:16:57,860
Some cause birth defects.

220
00:16:58,240 --> 00:16:59,540
Over the next few years,

221
00:16:59,940 --> 00:17:02,020
extensive efforts were made to clean up the neighborhood.

222
00:17:02,021 --> 00:17:09,460
We cleaned 65,000 lineal feet of storm sewers around that entire area.

223
00:17:09,461 --> 00:17:17,720
And of course, the hazardous waste byproducts did find their way underground to a nearby set of creeks,

224
00:17:17,820 --> 00:17:21,080
the Black and Burkholds Creeks that flow just north of Love Canal.

225
00:17:21,500 --> 00:17:27,980
We ended up testing the creek sediment and discovering that the same hazardous waste byproducts that were buried

226
00:17:27,981 --> 00:17:33,080
now have worked their way via water under the ground to the creek areas.

227
00:17:33,260 --> 00:17:37,240
One of the remedies were to actually dredge the creek.

228
00:17:38,260 --> 00:17:41,980
Crews dealing with the toxic dump itself decided that trying to remove,

229
00:17:42,240 --> 00:17:46,040
transport and store the chemicals somewhere else was inviting further disaster.

230
00:17:48,620 --> 00:17:51,260
The chemicals were going to have to stay where they were

231
00:17:51,261 --> 00:17:54,980
and a better cover and containment system put in place.

232
00:17:55,980 --> 00:18:01,960
The 99th Street School and 239 homes were demolished and placed on top of the canal,

233
00:18:02,700 --> 00:18:04,740
and then covered with clay.

234
00:18:05,740 --> 00:18:09,400
And over the top of it, we placed this synthetic liner,

235
00:18:09,940 --> 00:18:15,240
and this synthetic liner went directly over the entire 40-acre area, okay?

236
00:18:15,540 --> 00:18:20,520
And then after we placed the synthetic liner, we then installed this plastic liner.

237
00:18:20,521 --> 00:18:24,300
This liner goes over the entire 40-acre area.

238
00:18:24,301 --> 00:18:32,700
So when it rains, all of the precipitation will go through the ground and will actually hit the top of this liner, much like an umbrella.

239
00:18:33,020 --> 00:18:34,420
It will run off of it.

240
00:18:34,740 --> 00:18:38,020
The runoff is then collected in an underground barrier drain system.

241
00:18:39,000 --> 00:18:44,780
It's then pumped up to the facility at the top of the Love Canal, where it's treated by carbon filters.

242
00:18:45,640 --> 00:18:50,120
It is then discharged via storm sewer to the city of Niagara Falls as wastewater treatment plant,

243
00:18:50,121 --> 00:18:56,100
where it undergoes more filtration before clean water is discharged into the upper Niagara River.

244
00:18:57,320 --> 00:19:06,000
The capping and drainage system handles the material so well that anything that gets through is minimal.

245
00:19:06,760 --> 00:19:12,660
And we have a series of over 200 monitoring wells in the entire neighborhood

246
00:19:12,661 --> 00:19:18,280
to make sure that the groundwater is not being impacted by any of the contamination.

247
00:19:19,280 --> 00:19:24,980
It's been more than a quarter of a century since the Love Canal first became a notorious environmental disaster.

248
00:19:26,780 --> 00:19:29,000
New residents have been moving into the area.

249
00:19:29,940 --> 00:19:34,780
But many of those who lived there previously will never consider it safe.

250
00:19:36,220 --> 00:19:38,880
This is the earth. The earth shifts.

251
00:19:39,740 --> 00:19:46,240
And we saw what happened in the blizzard of 77 when we had the snow melt and the river rose.

252
00:19:46,241 --> 00:19:50,200
and that, they believe, created a lot of the Love Canal problem.

253
00:19:51,160 --> 00:19:53,640
It's foolish to think that they can contain it.

254
00:19:54,000 --> 00:19:56,540
The crisis at Love Canal created greater awareness

255
00:19:56,541 --> 00:19:59,120
of America's growing problem with industrial wastes.

256
00:19:59,800 --> 00:20:04,460
It was also primarily responsible for the creation of a federal program called Superfund.

257
00:20:05,340 --> 00:20:09,500
Since its inception in 1980, Superfund has spent more than $16 billion

258
00:20:09,501 --> 00:20:14,900
cleaning up over 2,000 hazardous waste sites around the United States,

259
00:20:14,901 --> 00:20:17,720
including the infamous Love Canal.

260
00:20:19,780 --> 00:20:25,940
Since 1978, more than $250 million has been spent on cleanup and containment

261
00:20:25,941 --> 00:20:28,400
of the contents of the Love Canal.

262
00:20:29,380 --> 00:20:32,500
Engineering Disasters will return on Modern Marvels.

263
00:20:33,300 --> 00:20:37,280
We now return to Engineering Disasters on Modern Marvels.

264
00:20:39,320 --> 00:20:40,800
Boston, Massachusetts.

265
00:20:41,560 --> 00:20:43,740
In the heart of this great American city

266
00:20:43,741 --> 00:20:46,500
lies a memorial embedded in the sidewalk.

267
00:20:47,980 --> 00:20:50,900
To a visitor, it may be of little interest,

268
00:20:51,700 --> 00:20:52,980
but to the longtime residents,

269
00:20:53,180 --> 00:20:54,940
it is a reminder of the deadly inferno

270
00:20:54,941 --> 00:20:56,800
at the Coconut Grove nightclub.

271
00:20:57,460 --> 00:20:59,580
Well, for disasters in the United States,

272
00:21:00,100 --> 00:21:05,080
the Coconut Grove up until the World Trade Center tragedy,

273
00:21:05,081 --> 00:21:08,720
the Coconut Grove drank second after the Iroquois Theater fire

274
00:21:08,721 --> 00:21:11,900
in Chicago in 1904 in terms of loss of life.

275
00:21:11,901 --> 00:21:17,380
Back in 1904 there, they had over 600 people were killed there.

276
00:21:17,480 --> 00:21:19,780
The Coconut Grove was 492 people,

277
00:21:20,260 --> 00:21:22,760
certainly the largest tragedy in the city of Boston.

278
00:21:24,300 --> 00:21:28,300
The night of November 28, 1942 was a cold night.

279
00:21:28,640 --> 00:21:31,340
It was during the war, so there was a lot of gloominess in the air.

280
00:21:31,860 --> 00:21:33,000
It was a chill in the air.

281
00:21:33,360 --> 00:21:36,080
And people were out to have a good time.

282
00:21:36,680 --> 00:21:40,680
And that night, there were, as best as we can figure,

283
00:21:40,681 --> 00:21:43,280
about a thousand people packed into that club.

284
00:21:43,720 --> 00:21:46,740
It was licensed for about 500 people.

285
00:21:47,480 --> 00:21:49,820
The nightclub was a multi-room facility

286
00:21:49,821 --> 00:21:52,220
that was originally built in a converted garage.

287
00:21:53,320 --> 00:21:57,260
It was a brick building with wooden timbers in the floors and in the ceilings,

288
00:21:57,640 --> 00:22:02,580
but the building itself was a good building to be used for a storage facility.

289
00:22:03,280 --> 00:22:06,020
They should have had sprinklers back then, but the lodge didn't require it.

290
00:22:06,860 --> 00:22:10,420
The club's decor was inspired by the Swank Coconut Grove Nightclub

291
00:22:10,421 --> 00:22:11,160
in Los Angeles.

292
00:22:12,120 --> 00:22:15,660
The Coconut Grove was designed to be a tropical paradise

293
00:22:15,661 --> 00:22:17,720
in the middle of chilly Boston.

294
00:22:17,960 --> 00:22:19,360
It had fake palm trees.

295
00:22:19,960 --> 00:22:24,400
It had distinct patterns in its chairs and on the walls.

296
00:22:24,820 --> 00:22:28,320
It had this kind of zebra pattern on a lot of the furniture.

297
00:22:28,620 --> 00:22:30,200
There were bongo drums.

298
00:22:30,460 --> 00:22:32,060
There was rattan furniture.

299
00:22:33,520 --> 00:22:35,380
From the standpoint of fire safety,

300
00:22:36,040 --> 00:22:37,740
the club's contents were a tinderbox

301
00:22:37,741 --> 00:22:39,320
of highly combustible materials.

302
00:22:40,420 --> 00:22:42,972
The fire began at about 10.15 p.m.

303
00:22:42,973 --> 00:22:48,600
in the Melody Lounge located in the building's basement and spread quickly.

304
00:22:49,660 --> 00:22:55,600
Its cause has most often been attributed to a busboy who lit a match too close to one of the synthetic palm trees.

305
00:22:57,800 --> 00:23:06,820
Jimmy Duggan, enjoying an evening with his newlywed wife and four friends, was sitting in the main dining room upstairs and sensed something was wrong.

306
00:23:07,380 --> 00:23:11,540
The few people that started coming up the stairs looked like they were going too fast for me.

307
00:23:12,700 --> 00:23:16,440
It looked like somebody's down there with a gun or a butcher knife.

308
00:23:18,100 --> 00:23:20,560
But you must remember, all this was going like that.

309
00:23:22,260 --> 00:23:25,000
Milliseconds later than that is when it started big time.

310
00:23:27,700 --> 00:23:32,880
The people rushing up from the downstairs lounge encountered the first of many exits,

311
00:23:33,300 --> 00:23:34,680
from which there was no escape.

312
00:23:34,681 --> 00:23:38,780
There was a door at the top of the stairs from the Melody Lounge to the outside.

313
00:23:39,540 --> 00:23:44,940
And this door was even equipped with a panic bar, the kind of crash bar you're supposed to push on it and you're supposed to push through.

314
00:23:45,420 --> 00:23:46,320
It was locked.

315
00:23:48,060 --> 00:23:53,720
Panic spread as quickly as the flames, and a flood of patrons raced for the revolving door at the front entrance.

316
00:23:54,980 --> 00:23:57,820
Some revolving doors are much larger than others.

317
00:23:58,660 --> 00:24:00,220
This happened to be a pretty small one.

318
00:24:00,221 --> 00:24:05,020
This revolving door, as I recall it, only lit at one person at a time.

319
00:24:05,640 --> 00:24:11,720
That's when I got all of us, all six of us, down on the floor

320
00:24:11,721 --> 00:24:14,900
and tell them to put their noses right on the floor

321
00:24:14,901 --> 00:24:17,600
because I could see them were not going to get out.

322
00:24:20,720 --> 00:24:23,060
First few people got through the doors that night,

323
00:24:23,061 --> 00:24:25,240
but immediately the doors jammed.

324
00:24:25,380 --> 00:24:26,780
A man was trapped in the doors.

325
00:24:27,540 --> 00:24:29,520
People came behind and piled up.

326
00:24:29,521 --> 00:24:32,920
Reportedly, the bodies piled up to about eight feet there that night.

327
00:24:33,380 --> 00:24:37,680
People climbing over others, trying to escape through that the only way they knew out.

328
00:24:38,240 --> 00:24:42,700
As the fire continued to spread, people throughout the club were trying to escape.

329
00:24:43,880 --> 00:24:45,700
Some exits were hidden by curtains.

330
00:24:46,360 --> 00:24:49,500
Others were locked to keep people from sneaking out without paying their bills.

331
00:24:51,020 --> 00:24:55,220
One door had been installed in a way that guaranteed casualties in a panic situation.

332
00:24:56,180 --> 00:25:03,580
It's tragic to see an inward opening door like existed in the Coconut Grove Broadway Street Lounge,

333
00:25:04,020 --> 00:25:09,140
which becomes blocks soon after the first few people who start pushing against that door.

334
00:25:09,420 --> 00:25:12,720
It's going to close shut. It stays shut. There's no way around it.

335
00:25:14,860 --> 00:25:18,200
Witnesses there that night report the fire coming in.

336
00:25:18,280 --> 00:25:20,520
They report a bluish flame, a yellow flame.

337
00:25:20,521 --> 00:25:24,300
it rolled right in, not in like a forest fire effect,

338
00:25:25,000 --> 00:25:27,560
eating on the satin draperies in the ceiling,

339
00:25:27,740 --> 00:25:30,680
dropping down into the palm trees, the bamboo.

340
00:25:32,500 --> 00:25:34,900
And I determined then at the very end

341
00:25:34,901 --> 00:25:37,500
that we're not going to get out of here.

342
00:25:39,240 --> 00:25:42,220
I took my jacket off and put it on my wife.

343
00:25:42,540 --> 00:25:43,840
I spread that over her back.

344
00:25:44,240 --> 00:25:45,180
It didn't take long.

345
00:25:45,660 --> 00:25:48,140
I was the last one of the group of the six

346
00:25:48,141 --> 00:25:50,240
that lost consciousness.

347
00:25:51,340 --> 00:25:55,840
It took only a few minutes for the fire to deplete the oxygen supply in the nightclub.

348
00:25:56,400 --> 00:26:02,000
Proof of that rapid consumption of the oxygen is the fact that the combustion of the materials,

349
00:26:02,080 --> 00:26:05,080
the actual burning of the materials present that night, was limited.

350
00:26:05,560 --> 00:26:08,260
There was not a total burnout of that Coconut Grove fire.

351
00:26:09,320 --> 00:26:13,480
Another fire earlier that evening had brought the Boston Fire Department into the neighborhood,

352
00:26:13,960 --> 00:26:15,440
enabling it to respond quickly.

353
00:26:16,040 --> 00:26:18,840
Fire was extinguished within a half hour.

354
00:26:18,841 --> 00:26:23,180
The department continued to rescue the victims, remove the victims.

355
00:26:23,960 --> 00:26:25,720
The police department was on scene.

356
00:26:26,260 --> 00:26:29,800
A lot of bystanders had come in now, a lot of soldiers, sailors.

357
00:26:30,280 --> 00:26:31,840
They were coming in, helping out.

358
00:26:31,940 --> 00:26:33,920
It was just overwhelming, the number of victims.

359
00:26:34,700 --> 00:26:38,540
Taxi cabs, police cars, any truck or vehicle available

360
00:26:38,541 --> 00:26:42,560
was pressed into service to transport these victims to the hospitals.

361
00:26:43,360 --> 00:26:48,080
A lot of people died from what seemed to be a mysterious gas.

362
00:26:48,081 --> 00:26:53,760
There were some people who had white foam on their lips, and the doctors were baffled by it.

363
00:26:53,780 --> 00:26:55,280
It looked like they had been gassed to death.

364
00:26:56,120 --> 00:27:00,960
It became evident that some of the gases emitted by the burning contents of the club must have been toxic.

365
00:27:01,960 --> 00:27:06,780
One of the materials that came into question soon after the fire was the leatherette,

366
00:27:07,340 --> 00:27:13,320
which was this false leather-resembling type material that was used in those days.

367
00:27:13,321 --> 00:27:23,960
We don't have samples that we can analyze by our methods today and inspect it and test it and fire test to see how well it performs.

368
00:27:24,360 --> 00:27:26,860
So this is part of the mystery of the Coconut Grove.

369
00:27:27,760 --> 00:27:33,660
Because the fire moved through the club so fast, it is thought one of the gases could have been acting as an accelerant.

370
00:27:34,860 --> 00:27:37,760
Fire seemed to have a momentum of its own.

371
00:27:37,761 --> 00:27:39,780
I can only equate it to a ramjet engine,

372
00:27:40,440 --> 00:27:43,400
where it just followed the oxygen, followed the fuel,

373
00:27:43,940 --> 00:27:46,920
and just went through that place with lightning speed.

374
00:27:47,620 --> 00:27:49,500
People were found in the new lounge,

375
00:27:50,240 --> 00:27:52,860
sitting at the bar with their drinks in front of them.

376
00:27:52,861 --> 00:27:55,080
When the firemen got in there, they told them,

377
00:27:55,280 --> 00:27:56,200
come on, get out of the room.

378
00:27:56,500 --> 00:27:58,940
And these people were dead, sitting in there at the tables,

379
00:27:59,380 --> 00:28:00,140
sitting at the bar.

380
00:28:02,780 --> 00:28:05,500
Many people who were there to help

381
00:28:05,501 --> 00:28:07,620
were forever haunted by what they saw.

382
00:28:07,760 --> 00:28:09,440
I mean, bodies were being pulled out of the club.

383
00:28:09,780 --> 00:28:12,300
There were so many bodies, they were being stacked like cordwood.

384
00:28:12,440 --> 00:28:15,300
I mean, this is the phrase you see over and over again with survivors.

385
00:28:15,420 --> 00:28:17,020
They stack the bodies like cordwood.

386
00:28:20,460 --> 00:28:22,880
Jimmy Duggan's wife was among the dead.

387
00:28:23,500 --> 00:28:24,820
Jimmy woke up in the hospital

388
00:28:24,821 --> 00:28:28,080
and began a long recovery from extensive burns.

389
00:28:29,140 --> 00:28:31,180
In the trial that followed the disaster,

390
00:28:31,181 --> 00:28:34,900
the owner of the Coconut Grove nightclub, Barney Walansky,

391
00:28:35,360 --> 00:28:37,320
was found guilty of involuntary manslaughter.

392
00:28:37,760 --> 00:28:45,320
The people who died and the people who suffered in the Coconut Grove fire did not do so in vain.

393
00:28:46,220 --> 00:28:53,860
We've been able to look back and see the improvements in fire testing and materials, medical advances,

394
00:28:54,640 --> 00:28:58,600
better understanding of disaster preparedness and disaster response.

395
00:28:59,400 --> 00:29:07,100
The devastating fire on November 28, 1942, was responsible for improvements in stricter enforcement of building codes

396
00:29:07,101 --> 00:29:08,073
across America.

397
00:29:08,074 --> 00:29:13,000
In Boston, particular attention was paid to the safety code stipulating that

398
00:29:13,001 --> 00:29:19,620
all revolving doors should be flanked by hinged doors that swing open outward. There

399
00:29:19,621 --> 00:29:24,680
is another law in Boston that reveals the lasting psychological effect of this disaster.

400
00:29:26,100 --> 00:29:29,820
It is illegal to name a new nightclub, the Coconut Grove.

401
00:29:32,580 --> 00:29:36,720
Burn victims from the Coconut Grove fire were among the first patients to be treated

402
00:29:36,721 --> 00:29:39,580
with a new antibiotic, penicillin.

403
00:29:40,820 --> 00:29:43,540
Engineering Disasters will return on Modern Marvels.

404
00:29:44,660 --> 00:29:48,360
We now return to Engineering Disasters on Modern Marvels.

405
00:29:49,240 --> 00:29:49,940
Computers.

406
00:29:50,440 --> 00:29:54,120
We've all come to accept the often frustrating problems that go with them.

407
00:29:55,180 --> 00:29:57,300
Bugs, viruses, and software glitches

408
00:29:57,301 --> 00:30:00,060
are small prices to pay for the convenience.

409
00:30:00,900 --> 00:30:04,660
But in high-tech and high-speed military and space program applications,

410
00:30:04,661 --> 00:30:09,000
The smallest problem can be magnified into disaster.

411
00:30:10,700 --> 00:30:16,440
On June 4, 1996, scientists prepared to launch an unmanned Ariane 5 rocket.

412
00:30:16,820 --> 00:30:23,420
It was carrying scientific satellites designed to establish precisely how the Earth's magnetic field interacts with solar winds.

413
00:30:24,780 --> 00:30:31,380
The rocket was built for the European Space Agency and lifted off from its facility on the coast of French Guiana.

414
00:30:32,260 --> 00:30:36,840
At about 37 seconds into the flight, they first noticed something was going wrong,

415
00:30:37,020 --> 00:30:39,760
that the nozzles were swiveling in a way they really shouldn't.

416
00:30:40,180 --> 00:30:44,160
Around 40 seconds into the flight, clearly the vehicle was in trouble,

417
00:30:44,161 --> 00:30:46,440
and that's when they made the decision to destroy it.

418
00:30:46,820 --> 00:30:50,260
The range safety officer, with tremendous guts, pressed the button,

419
00:30:50,700 --> 00:30:54,900
blew up the rocket before it could become a hazard to public safety.

420
00:30:55,780 --> 00:30:58,440
This was the maiden voyage of the Ariane 5,

421
00:30:58,441 --> 00:31:03,320
and its destruction took place because of a flaw embedded in the rocket's software.

422
00:31:04,040 --> 00:31:08,060
The problem on the Arian was that there was a number that required 64 bits to express

423
00:31:08,061 --> 00:31:10,960
and they wanted to convert it to a 16-bit number.

424
00:31:11,480 --> 00:31:14,720
They assumed that the number was never going to be very big,

425
00:31:14,800 --> 00:31:17,600
that most of those digits in the 64-bit number were zeros.

426
00:31:18,680 --> 00:31:19,520
They were wrong.

427
00:31:19,900 --> 00:31:24,820
The inability of one software program to accept the kind of number generated by another

428
00:31:24,821 --> 00:31:26,680
was at the root of the failure.

429
00:31:27,480 --> 00:31:30,760
Software development had become a very costly part of new technology.

430
00:31:31,860 --> 00:31:34,460
The Ariane 4 rocket had been very successful,

431
00:31:35,000 --> 00:31:38,600
so much of the software created for it was also used in the Ariane 5.

432
00:31:39,260 --> 00:31:45,080
The basic problem was that the Ariane 5 was faster, accelerated faster,

433
00:31:45,800 --> 00:31:48,800
and the software hadn't accounted for that.

434
00:31:48,801 --> 00:31:52,100
The destruction of the rocket was a huge financial disaster,

435
00:31:52,840 --> 00:31:55,080
all due to a minute software error.

436
00:31:56,080 --> 00:32:00,820
But this wasn't the first time data conversion problems had plagued modern rocket technology.

437
00:32:02,220 --> 00:32:05,560
In 1991, with the start of the first Gulf War,

438
00:32:06,020 --> 00:32:10,060
the Patriot missile experienced a similar kind of number conversion problem.

439
00:32:10,720 --> 00:32:16,440
And as a result, 28 people, 28 American soldiers were killed and about 100 others wounded

440
00:32:16,441 --> 00:32:22,540
when the Patriot, which was supposed to protect against incoming scuds, failed to fire a missile.

441
00:32:23,320 --> 00:32:28,240
When Iraq invaded Kuwait and America launched Desert Storm in early 1991,

442
00:32:29,620 --> 00:32:33,160
Patriot missile batteries were deployed to protect Saudi Arabia and Israel

443
00:32:33,161 --> 00:32:35,400
from Iraqi Scud missile attacks.

444
00:32:36,620 --> 00:32:40,260
The Patriot is a U.S. medium-range surface-to-air system

445
00:32:40,261 --> 00:32:42,760
manufactured by the Raytheon Company.

446
00:32:44,180 --> 00:32:49,420
The size of the Patriot interceptor itself is about roughly 20 feet long,

447
00:32:49,780 --> 00:32:52,220
and it weighs about 2,000 pounds.

448
00:32:52,540 --> 00:32:57,140
And it carries a warhead of about, I think it's roughly 150 pounds.

449
00:32:57,280 --> 00:33:04,240
And the warhead itself is a high explosive, which has fragments around it.

450
00:33:04,660 --> 00:33:09,120
The casing of the warhead is designed to act like buckshot.

451
00:33:09,520 --> 00:33:13,800
The missiles are carried four per container and are transported by a semi-trailer.

452
00:33:14,540 --> 00:33:22,140
The Patriot anti-missile system goes back at least 20 years now.

453
00:33:22,540 --> 00:33:28,120
It was originally designed as an air defense missile to shoot down enemy airplanes.

454
00:33:28,580 --> 00:33:31,640
In the first Gulf War, when that war came along,

455
00:33:32,360 --> 00:33:36,860
the Army wanted to use it to shoot down scuds, not airplanes.

456
00:33:37,740 --> 00:33:40,780
The Iraqi Air Force was not so much of a problem,

457
00:33:41,320 --> 00:33:43,720
but the Army was worried about scuds.

458
00:33:44,100 --> 00:33:47,420
And so they tried to upgrade the Patriot.

459
00:33:48,100 --> 00:33:52,120
Intercepting an enemy missile traveling at Mach 5 was going to be challenging enough.

460
00:33:52,540 --> 00:33:59,540
But when the Patriot was rushed into service, the Army was not aware of an Iraqi modification

461
00:33:59,541 --> 00:34:03,540
that made their Scuds nearly impossible to hit.

462
00:34:03,541 --> 00:34:09,540
What happened is the Scuds that were coming in were unstable, they were wobbly.

463
00:34:09,541 --> 00:34:16,540
The reason for this was the Iraqis, in order to get 600 kilometers out of a 300 kilometer range missile,

464
00:34:16,541 --> 00:34:20,540
took weight out of the front warhead. They made the warhead lighter.

465
00:34:20,541 --> 00:34:30,160
So now the Patriot's trying to come at the Scud, and most of the time, the overwhelming majority of the time, it would just fly by the Scud.

466
00:34:30,740 --> 00:34:40,142
Once the Patriot system operators realized the Patriot missed its target, they detonated the Patriot's warhead to avoid possible casualties if it was allowed to fall

467
00:34:40,143 --> 00:34:40,940
to the ground.

468
00:34:40,941 --> 00:34:51,920
That was what most people saw as big fireballs in the sky and misunderstood as intercepts of Scud warheads.

469
00:34:52,200 --> 00:34:56,380
Although in the night skies, Patriots appeared to be successfully destroying Scuds,

470
00:34:57,100 --> 00:35:00,080
at Daharan, there could be no mistake about its performance.

471
00:35:01,240 --> 00:35:08,460
There, the Patriots' radar system lost track of an incoming Scud and never launched due to a software flaw.

472
00:35:10,940 --> 00:35:15,280
It was the Israelis who first discovered that the longer the system was on,

473
00:35:16,360 --> 00:35:21,580
the greater the time discrepancy became, due to a clock embedded in the system's computer.

474
00:35:22,680 --> 00:35:25,680
About two weeks before the tragedy in Dara'in,

475
00:35:26,080 --> 00:35:30,280
the Israelis reported to the Defense Department that the system was losing time.

476
00:35:30,281 --> 00:35:35,060
After about eight hours of running, they noticed that the system was becoming noticeably less accurate.

477
00:35:35,660 --> 00:35:39,360
The Defense Department responded by telling all of the Patriot batteries

478
00:35:39,361 --> 00:35:42,100
to not leave the systems on for a long time.

479
00:35:42,720 --> 00:35:44,200
They never said what a long time was.

480
00:35:44,880 --> 00:35:46,620
Eight hours, ten hours, a thousand hours.

481
00:35:47,000 --> 00:35:47,700
Nobody knew.

482
00:35:48,900 --> 00:35:51,820
The Patriot battery stationed at the barracks at Daharan

483
00:35:51,821 --> 00:35:53,900
and its flawed internal clock

484
00:35:53,901 --> 00:35:58,020
had been on over a hundred hours on the night of February 25th.

485
00:35:59,220 --> 00:36:02,860
It tracked time to an accuracy of about a tenth of a second.

486
00:36:03,920 --> 00:36:05,860
Now, a tenth of a second is an interesting number

487
00:36:05,861 --> 00:36:08,820
because it can't be expressed in binary exactly.

488
00:36:09,360 --> 00:36:13,520
which means it can't be expressed exactly in any modern digital computer.

489
00:36:14,160 --> 00:36:17,900
It's hard to believe, but use this as an example.

490
00:36:18,980 --> 00:36:20,220
Let's take the number one-third.

491
00:36:21,120 --> 00:36:24,620
One-third cannot be expressed in decimal exactly.

492
00:36:25,300 --> 00:36:28,520
One-third is .333 going on for infinity.

493
00:36:29,660 --> 00:36:33,980
There's no way to do that with absolute accuracy in decimal.

494
00:36:34,320 --> 00:36:37,180
That's exactly the same kind of problem that happened in the Patriot.

495
00:36:37,181 --> 00:36:41,120
The longer the system ran, the worse the time error became.

496
00:36:41,860 --> 00:36:47,320
After 100 hours of operation, the error in time was only about one-third of a second.

497
00:36:48,180 --> 00:36:51,120
But in terms of targeting a missile traveling at Mach 5,

498
00:36:51,940 --> 00:36:55,620
it resulted in a tracking error of over 600 meters.

499
00:36:56,440 --> 00:36:59,260
It would be a fatal error for the soldiers at Daharan.

500
00:36:59,800 --> 00:37:06,520
What happened is a Scud launch was detected by early warning satellites,

501
00:37:06,521 --> 00:37:10,560
and they knew that the Scud was coming in their general direction.

502
00:37:10,700 --> 00:37:12,020
They didn't know where it was coming.

503
00:37:12,740 --> 00:37:15,900
It was now up to the radar component of the Patriot system,

504
00:37:16,220 --> 00:37:17,080
defending Daharan,

505
00:37:17,760 --> 00:37:20,260
to locate and keep track of the incoming enemy missile.

506
00:37:21,000 --> 00:37:22,320
The radar was very smart.

507
00:37:22,720 --> 00:37:24,420
It would actually track the position of the Scud

508
00:37:24,421 --> 00:37:26,920
and then predict where it probably would be

509
00:37:26,921 --> 00:37:28,840
the next time the radar sent a pulse out.

510
00:37:29,280 --> 00:37:30,560
That was called the range gate.

511
00:37:30,960 --> 00:37:34,060
Then, once the Patriot decides

512
00:37:34,061 --> 00:37:36,440
enough time has passed to go back

513
00:37:36,441 --> 00:37:41,060
and check the next location for this detected object, it goes back.

514
00:37:41,460 --> 00:37:45,740
So when it went back to the wrong place, it then sees no object,

515
00:37:46,340 --> 00:37:51,120
and it decides that there was no object that was a false detection, and drops the track.

516
00:37:51,640 --> 00:37:54,380
The incoming Scud disappeared from the radar screen,

517
00:37:54,880 --> 00:37:57,420
and seconds later, it slammed into the barracks.

518
00:37:57,600 --> 00:38:03,020
The Scud killed 28, and was the last one fired during the first Gulf War.

519
00:38:03,021 --> 00:38:08,720
Tragically, the updated software arrived at Daharan the following day.

520
00:38:09,900 --> 00:38:15,720
The software flaw had been fixed, closing one chapter in the troubled history of the Patriot missile.

521
00:38:18,800 --> 00:38:24,000
Patriot is actually an acronym for Phased Array Tracking Intercept of Target.

522
00:38:25,580 --> 00:38:28,560
Engineering Disasters will return on Modern Marvels.

523
00:38:28,561 --> 00:38:33,560
We now return to Engineering Disasters on Modern Marvels.

524
00:38:34,560 --> 00:38:38,740
Architects and building engineers are endlessly refining the technology of construction.

525
00:38:40,200 --> 00:38:45,980
But man's penchant for erecting large structures has resulted in the development of another engineering skill.

526
00:38:47,440 --> 00:38:49,680
Building demolition by implosion.

527
00:38:49,681 --> 00:38:58,920
for nearly a century with some strategically placed charges and the push of a plunger

528
00:38:58,921 --> 00:39:02,520
building after building has collapsed on cue

529
00:39:02,521 --> 00:39:12,500
an implosion of a building is a more efficient function than a proper way of doing it

530
00:39:12,501 --> 00:39:18,060
when you do the hammer and ball it takes a great deal of time and it can be very sloppy

531
00:39:18,061 --> 00:39:22,060
A controlled implosion usually uses the laws of physics.

532
00:39:22,400 --> 00:39:26,740
So it's a controlled explosion, and the building comes down in one big heap,

533
00:39:26,840 --> 00:39:29,260
and you can usually just truck out the material.

534
00:39:33,320 --> 00:39:38,700
The term implosion is often used, but scientifically speaking, it's not an implosion,

535
00:39:39,140 --> 00:39:44,400
but rather a set of carefully planned explosions that cause the building to fall in on itself.

536
00:39:44,401 --> 00:39:48,660
generally to bring down a building

537
00:39:48,661 --> 00:39:52,940
blasters will explode the major support columns on the lower floors first

538
00:39:52,941 --> 00:39:57,920
and a few of the upper stories in taller buildings different explosives are used

539
00:39:57,921 --> 00:39:59,120
for different materials

540
00:39:59,121 --> 00:40:04,600
when done properly it's the safest and fastest way to level a multi-story

541
00:40:04,601 --> 00:40:11,600
structure

542
00:40:11,601 --> 00:40:17,480
But in May of 1989, one building refused to be brought to its knees.

543
00:40:20,200 --> 00:40:31,600
In Providence, Rhode Island, a 10-story public housing structure stood its ground, not only once, but for a second time.

544
00:40:32,660 --> 00:40:35,660
It was a most curious sort of engineering disaster.

545
00:40:36,580 --> 00:40:39,820
A local company, Bill Ray Demolition, was in charge

546
00:40:39,821 --> 00:40:42,420
and brought in New York implosion specialists

547
00:40:42,421 --> 00:40:46,580
to bring down the building as part of a multi-million dollar modernization of the area.

548
00:40:47,440 --> 00:40:51,160
The buildings in the housing project to be destroyed were built in the 50s,

549
00:40:51,540 --> 00:40:55,460
and to the original architect's credit, turned out to be sturdier than expected.

550
00:40:56,580 --> 00:41:00,640
The buildings were constructed with poured concrete and rebar

551
00:41:00,641 --> 00:41:05,100
using a honeycomb design, which sort of reinforced themselves.

552
00:41:05,660 --> 00:41:10,020
As the demolition team prepared the building, excitement built in Providence.

553
00:41:10,740 --> 00:41:15,900
And in preparing the building, because they drilled thousands of holes in the first and second floor,

554
00:41:16,260 --> 00:41:19,980
took out doors and windows that existed and drilled these holes.

555
00:41:19,981 --> 00:41:25,400
And connecting with detonation cord, they put in around a quarter pound of dynamite.

556
00:41:26,940 --> 00:41:32,420
650 pounds of explosives were embedded in the steel-reinforced concrete on the first and second floors

557
00:41:32,421 --> 00:41:35,480
that provided support for the T-shaped structure.

558
00:41:35,660 --> 00:41:40,120
Early on the morning of May 21st, a warning horn sounded.

559
00:41:41,060 --> 00:41:45,260
And a few minutes later at 8.30 a.m., the plunger was pushed.

560
00:41:48,380 --> 00:41:52,700
There was a huge explosion, rocked the general area,

561
00:41:53,240 --> 00:41:57,000
and a big cloud of smoke came up that engulfed the entire building.

562
00:41:57,001 --> 00:42:01,880
The only thing I remember seeing is two seagulls on the roof of this 10-story high-rise.

563
00:42:02,280 --> 00:42:05,200
They remained in sight throughout the implosion.

564
00:42:05,660 --> 00:42:08,160
Then we noticed that the building hadn't even moved.

565
00:42:08,420 --> 00:42:11,660
It was kind of embarrassing to both the explosive company and my father's company

566
00:42:11,661 --> 00:42:14,540
because, you know, we're out there experiencing this field

567
00:42:14,541 --> 00:42:16,560
and we do it every day of our lives, and all of a sudden,

568
00:42:17,560 --> 00:42:19,580
failure just happens in front of you, in front of your eyes.

569
00:42:20,700 --> 00:42:22,320
And it was just embarrassing.

570
00:42:22,860 --> 00:42:25,760
Well, after the first demolition implosion failed,

571
00:42:26,240 --> 00:42:29,960
we conducted a planning meeting with all the public safety officials

572
00:42:29,961 --> 00:42:31,900
and the implosion specialists

573
00:42:31,901 --> 00:42:34,720
and determined that what was holding the building up

574
00:42:34,721 --> 00:42:38,400
was the heavy end stairwells for the building.

575
00:42:40,160 --> 00:42:42,480
After setting new charges in the stairwells,

576
00:42:42,640 --> 00:42:45,440
at 1.30 p.m., the plunger came down a second time.

577
00:42:48,620 --> 00:42:50,360
And when that second charge went off

578
00:42:50,361 --> 00:42:52,100
and that building started to fall,

579
00:42:52,680 --> 00:42:54,200
you had the excitement thinking it was going to go.

580
00:42:54,880 --> 00:42:57,320
And all of a sudden, it just got bombed up between each other and stood there.

581
00:43:04,720 --> 00:43:15,348
Well, there seemed to be a great deal of laughter from the crowd, a great deal of befuddlement on behalf of the staff and the project managers.

582
00:43:15,349 --> 00:43:17,280
We were very disappointed.

583
00:43:18,340 --> 00:43:22,980
The building had fallen about 30 feet and was leaning at a 20 degree angle.

584
00:43:23,540 --> 00:43:27,859
You had a 10 story high rise leaning towards the public streets.

585
00:43:27,860 --> 00:43:31,300
So it was a disaster. It was quite scary, actually.

586
00:43:31,680 --> 00:43:34,340
They knew at that point that a third set of charges wouldn't work

587
00:43:34,341 --> 00:43:38,040
because of the way the structure was now collapsed and leaning into each other.

588
00:43:38,640 --> 00:43:39,740
There was no way for it to fall.

589
00:43:40,600 --> 00:43:43,700
So they started voicing their other options, and that's when they had meetings

590
00:43:43,701 --> 00:43:46,600
and came to the conclusion that it had to come down with a wrecking ball.

591
00:43:47,520 --> 00:43:51,540
What was supposed to take seconds now is going to take several weeks.

592
00:43:52,180 --> 00:43:55,480
Streets had to stay closed and homes and businesses evacuated

593
00:43:55,481 --> 00:43:58,860
until the remains of the leaning building were deemed safe.

594
00:43:58,861 --> 00:44:03,760
Slowly, the building was pounded into submission without further incident.

595
00:44:04,640 --> 00:44:07,060
But the failed implosion was the talk of the town.

596
00:44:07,720 --> 00:44:13,220
Well, in good American spirit, entrepreneurial spirit, we tried to make lemonade with the lemons,

597
00:44:13,340 --> 00:44:14,600
even though they were sour lemons.

598
00:44:14,780 --> 00:44:19,060
What happened is a number of people were taking photographs.

599
00:44:20,060 --> 00:44:24,860
Someone talked about the Tower of Pisa, so we said now we have the Leaning Tower of Providence.

600
00:44:24,861 --> 00:44:30,820
So we made some t-shirts up and actually made some money on the event.

601
00:44:33,780 --> 00:44:37,300
Today there are a series of low-rise public housing units

602
00:44:37,301 --> 00:44:42,400
where the Leaning Tower of Providence defiantly once stood.

